  planner_response {
    response: "I have upgraded the Blueberrys Caf\303\251 experience using the core cinematic principles from MengTo\'s Kage project. Rather than a static landing page, the site now behaves as a deep, layered environment that you move *through* as you scroll.\n\nHere is an overview of the upgrades:\n\n*   **Atmospheric Particle System**: Implemented a procedural HTML5 Canvas layer (`AtmosphericParticles`) that renders soft, softly illuminated petals and floral dust drifting continuously across the viewport, mimicking the floating leaves in the Kage environment.\n*   **Deep Layered Parallax (2.5D Camera)**: The **Hero** and **Caf\303\251 Experience** sections have been re-engineered. Instead of standard flat scrolling, they now feature intense Z-axis scaling. Foreground architectural frames and floral cutouts move aggressively toward the camera, while the deep background scales slowly, simulating the feeling of a WebGL camera pushing physically deeper into the caf\303\251.\n*   **Cinematic Section Transitions**: Introduced a `CinematicSection` wrapper across the layout. As you scroll into new chapters (like *The Blueberrys World* or *The Garden*), the sections dynamically unblur (`filter: blur(10px) \342\236\224 0px`) and fade in, perfectly matching Kage\'s optical transition system.\n*   **Custom Interactive Cursor**: Replaced the default desktop pointer with a custom, fluid spring-animated ring cursor that expands and snaps smoothly when hovering over interactive editorial elements. \n\nThe application now feels like a highly polished, interactive art book layered seamlessly over a living, breathing environment."
    thinking: "**Developing Immersive Elements**\n\nI\'ve made exciting progress. I\'ve successfully implemented an immersive particle layer using `AtmosphericParticles.tsx`, creating softly falling, spinning petals for visual enhancement.\n\n\n**Implementing Visual Depth**\n\nI\'ve just added a custom expanding circular cursor, dynamically reacting to interactive elements. The default cursor is now completely hidden on desktop. Also, cinematic transitions now use `useScroll` to apply optical blur and opacity fades between sections, as per the specifications. Additionally, the hero and cafe experience sections now utilize deep WebGL-style 2.5D parallax with layered alphas, scaling cutouts and lines to produce a far more immersive experience.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I have upgraded the Blueberrys Caf\303\251 experience using the core cinematic principles from MengTo\'s Kage project. Rather than a static landing page, the site now behaves as a deep, layered environment that you move *through* as you scroll.\n\nHere is an overview of the upgrades:\n\n*   **Atmospheric Particle System**: Implemented a procedural HTML5 Canvas layer (`AtmosphericParticles`) that renders soft, softly illuminated petals and floral dust drifting continuously across the viewport, mimicking the floating leaves in the Kage environment.\n*   **Deep Layered Parallax (2.5D Camera)**: The **Hero** and **Caf\303\251 Experience** sections have been re-engineered. Instead of standard flat scrolling, they now feature intense Z-axis scaling. Foreground architectural frames and floral cutouts move aggressively toward the camera, while the deep background scales slowly, simulating the feeling of a WebGL camera pushing physically deeper into the caf\303\251.\n*   **Cinematic Section Transitions**: Introduced a `CinematicSection` wrapper across the layout. As you scroll into new chapters (like *The Blueberrys World* or *The Garden*), the sections dynamically unblur (`filter: blur(10px) \342\236\224 0px`) and fade in, perfectly matching Kage\'s optical transition system.\n*   **Custom Interactive Cursor**: Replaced the default desktop pointer with a custom, fluid spring-animated ring cursor that expands and snaps smoothly when hovering over interactive editorial elements. \n\nThe application now feels like a highly polished, interactive art book layered seamlessly over a living, breathing environment."
