  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Project Scope**\n\nOkay, I\'m defining the project\'s parameters now. The objective is clear: build a nice page transition with Framer Motion that feels like turning a page in a floral journal. Navigational elements will trigger the effect, so that\342\200\231s a key design constraint. This is all coming together; I\'m pretty pleased.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Implementation**\n\nNow, I\'m analyzing implementation strategies. Considering this single-page scrolling structure, simulating page transitions means creating overlay effects that synchronize with scrolling. I\'m leaning toward an overlay that sweeps across. I also need to make sure the app entrance looks good. I think overlapping sheets could be the most effective solution, maybe with a floral graphic. When the nav link is clicked, the overlay would activate, immediately scroll, and then fade.\n\n\n**Refining the Animation Logic**\n\nOkay, I\'m now refining the animation flow. I\'m going to create a `PageTransition` component, and am leaning toward using a `TransitionContext` to wrap the app and manage the transition state. This context will expose a `navigate(targetId)` function. The animation logic is becoming clearer; a `motion.div` fixed to the viewport will simulate the page turn, using `scaleX` and `originX` properties to control the \"flipping\" effect. After the page closes, it will scroll, then open again. I\'m going to use a `bg-cafe-cream` color, and am playing with the idea of a subtle floral graphic or a curved edge.\n\n\n**Developing the Overlay Effect**\n\nI\'m now refining the visual overlay, and I\'ve decided on a dual-layer effect: a pink layer followed quickly by a cream layer to enhance the page transition. I\'ve also outlined the required step-by-step code changes, to create a context, wrap the app, and update the navigation and anchor links to use the context. I\'m thinking more clearly now about animation implementation, leaning toward `scaleX` for a reliable page turn, likely sweeping right to left.\n\n\n**Visualizing the Transition**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the visual details. An SVG clip-path animation or layered sweep is looking very promising, even premium. I\'m imagining a dual-layer overlay with `cafe-teal` or `cafe-pink` as the accent color, followed by `cafe-ivory` with a floral texture, or a solid color if that\'s easier. The animation sequence is planned: a link click triggers a right-to-left sweep in, scroll happens, and then the overlay sweeps out left to right.\n\n\n**Implementing the Context**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `TransitionContext`. I\'ve added a `navigateTo` function to handle the transition logic. This involves managing `isTransitioning` state, delaying the scroll to synchronize with the transition animation, and ensuring a smooth transition to the target element using Lenis if it\'s available, and scrollIntoView otherwise. The plan is to have a short delay before scrolling, followed by a very short delay to open the new page, now that `PageTransitionOverlay` will be rendered conditionally within `AnimatePresence`.\n\n\n**Refining the Component Design**\n\nOkay, I\'m now refining the visual overlay component itself. I\'m focusing on the `PageTransitionOverlay`, now I\'m building a physical journal page with two layers. The pink layer will set the tone, and then the cafe-cream layer will simulate the page itself, complete with a shadow for added realism. I\'m imagining a motion.div element, with a fixed position, and will use `scaleX` for the flipping effect, with a nice ease. The design is coming together, and I\'m very pleased with the aesthetic.\n\n\n\n**Finalizing the Page Element**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the visual element that simulates the journal page turn. I\'m focusing on refining the `PageTransitionOverlay` component now, and I\'ve settled on the `motion.div` element with specific key properties: the initial `scaleX` is set to 0. I\'ve defined `animate` to set it to 1, and the `exit` transition sets it back to 0. I\'m using an ease with a duration of 0.8 and a delay of 0.1, to give a realistic page turning simulation. I have built the floral decorative element, with specific motion properties; its initial opacity and rotation are set to zero, and the animated opacity and rotation is defined, to simulate emergence, with a delay. Now I need to update the links.\nI know the code needs an update; specifically, in `src/components/Navigation.tsx` for both desktop and mobile versions.\n\n\n**Updating Link Interactions**\n\nNow, I\'m integrating the overlay animation with navigation links in various components, including Hero, LocationSection, and Footer, to trigger the page transition effect. I\'m focusing on handling internal vs. external links; external links should behave normally. I\'ve updated the `handleNavigation` function within the `TransitionProvider` to check the `href` attribute, ensuring that internal links trigger the transition and scrolling, while external links are opened in the standard way. I\'m still feeling very good about my plan!\n\n\n\n**Refining the Page Element**\n\nI\'m now refining the visual properties of the `PageTransitionOverlay` component. I\'ve broken the page transition effect into two layers: a pink layer for emphasis, and a textured cream layer that simulates the journal page. The initial `scaleX` is set to 0. I\'ve set up the animation to transition the `scaleX` to 1, then back to 0. I\'ve also set up a floral decorative element; its initial opacity and rotation are set to zero, and the animated opacity and rotation are defined, to simulate its emergence, with a delay. Now I\'ll implement these values!\n\n\n**Enhancing the Aesthetic**\n\nI\'m now enhancing the aesthetic of the page turn animation, and am focused on fine-tuning the visual effect. I\'m adding a blur and scale to the `PageTransitionOverlay` with `motion.div` for a softer appearance during the transition and am experimenting with a floral decorative element for the page. I\'m excited; this feels right!\n\n\n\n**Defining the Workflow**\n\nNow, I\'m defining the necessary files and considering where to make updates. I\'ll create `TransitionContext.tsx`, and I also need to update `App.tsx`, and various navigation components, like `Navigation.tsx`, `Hero.tsx`, and others. This feels like the right place! I need to ensure links like `#our-cafe`, `#menu`, etc. trigger the transition. I\'ll need to figure out what to do with the menu.\n\n\n\n**Confirming Library Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the `useLenis` hook for smoother scrolling. Checking that I can use the `useLenis` hook from the `lenis/react` library, which is already installed. I\'ve verified that the context setup is complete and ready to utilize this. Now it\'s time to refine the user experience with this component.\n\n\n**Refining the Context Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the logic within `TransitionContext.tsx`, and I\'ve focused on ensuring the `navigateTo` function manages the `isTransitioning` state, preventing rapid transitions. The focus is on a smooth transition between content, I\'m setting a delay for page turn animation duration and a scroll. I\'m leveraging the `useLenis` hook for smoother scrolling, and the scrolling logic now ensures a short delay, and that `scrollTo` is invoked. I\'m confident about these changes.\n\n\n\n**Implementing the AnimatePresence**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `AnimatePresence` component, wrapping the `PageTransitionOverlay` within the `TransitionContext` to manage the conditional rendering. I\'ve set up the context provider with `navigateTo` function, and am focusing on setting up the two-layer overlay, including the pink and cream layers. I feel I am making good progress.\n\n\n\n**Optimizing the Component**\n\nI\'m now implementing visual enhancements to the `PageTransitionOverlay` component. I\'m focusing on adding a subtle background texture and refining the animation timings. The animated element is now fully realized, with a floral graphic that will emerge in the cream-colored page turn. The animations are looking very realistic and natural, and now I just need to connect the dots.\n\n\n\n**Refining the Scroll Logic**\n\nI\'m now integrating the `TransitionContext` with my navigation. Specifically, I\'m updating my navigation components to use the new hook and manage internal links. The implementation in `Navigation.tsx` now uses `useTransition` for desktop links, and I\'ve ensured a smooth transition to top with Lenis for the root or top of the page. I\'m verifying the scroll behavior after transitions.\n\n\n\n**Finalizing the Scroll Behavior**\n\nOkay, I\'m now finishing up the scroll logic. I\'ve updated `Navigation.tsx`, `Hero.tsx`, and `LocationSection.tsx` to handle navigation clicks using the `TransitionContext`. Internal links now smoothly trigger transitions using the `navigateTo` function. I\'ve updated the `handleNavigation` function for better external link handling. I\'m making sure Lenis is used for smooth scrolling. I\'m now testing.\n\n\n**Implementing the Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the updates to the code and updating the `App.tsx` file to wrap the application within the `TransitionProvider` and `ReactLenis` for managing transitions and smooth scrolling. I am updating navigation components, like `Navigation.tsx` and `Hero.tsx`, to integrate the new context and handle navigation clicks. I\'ve got the page transition overlay component design and aesthetics locked down, and I\'m ready to begin adding the cream paper textures and shadow effects.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 40
      nanos: 784898410
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
