  planner_response {
    response: "I have implemented an elegant, physical-journal-style page transition effect for all major navigational interactions, utilizing Framer Motion and React Context.\n\nHere is an overview of the design additions:\n\n*   **Global Transition Context (`TransitionContext.tsx`)**: Created a dedicated context provider to intercept navigation requests (like clicking the \"Menu\" or \"Reserve\" links). Instead of instantly snapping to the section, it orchestrates a synchronized animation sequence.\n*   **The \"Journal Page Turn\" Effect**: When a link is clicked, a two-stage animated overlay sweeps across the screen from the right to the left. \n    *   **Layer 1**: A swift, deep accent pink (`bg-cafe-pink`) sweep acts as the edge of the turning page.\n    *   **Layer 2**: Followed immediately by a textured cream paper layer. We applied a multiply-blended paper texture to this layer, perfectly mimicking the tactile feel of an art book or journal.\n*   **Floral Centerpiece Detail**: While the \"page\" is actively turning across your screen (and the site scrolls underneath it), a subtle, faded leaf emblem encased in a delicate border appears in the center, labeled gently with the word \"Chapter\" to enhance the physical reading experience.\n*   **Synchronized Lenis Scrolling**: The smooth scrolling (`ReactLenis`) logic is now integrated into this transition timing. The engine waits until the screen is fully obscured by the new page before initiating the scroll, ensuring you never see a jarring jump\342\200\224only a smooth reveal as the new page completes its turn. \n\nAll main navigational anchors across the navigation bar, hero buttons, and footer links have been wired into this new transition system."
    thinking: "**Assessing Page Transition Aesthetics**\n\nI\'ve successfully compiled the code and now am focusing on the user\'s aesthetic goal. My priority is to create an elegant Framer Motion transition effect, which mimics the experience of flipping to a new page in a floral journal. I\'m keen to nail that tactile, physical feeling the user seeks.\n\n\n**Confirming Technical Implementation**\n\nI\'ve confirmed the implementation aligns perfectly with the aesthetic goals. The `PageTransitionOverlay` component precisely renders the desired deep pink sweep with the ivory paper texture and leaf icon. The mobile menu integration in `Navigation.tsx` neatly closes the menu and initiates the transition, and `TransitionProvider` wraps the content as expected. No import issues were found.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I have implemented an elegant, physical-journal-style page transition effect for all major navigational interactions, utilizing Framer Motion and React Context.\n\nHere is an overview of the design additions:\n\n*   **Global Transition Context (`TransitionContext.tsx`)**: Created a dedicated context provider to intercept navigation requests (like clicking the \"Menu\" or \"Reserve\" links). Instead of instantly snapping to the section, it orchestrates a synchronized animation sequence.\n*   **The \"Journal Page Turn\" Effect**: When a link is clicked, a two-stage animated overlay sweeps across the screen from the right to the left. \n    *   **Layer 1**: A swift, deep accent pink (`bg-cafe-pink`) sweep acts as the edge of the turning page.\n    *   **Layer 2**: Followed immediately by a textured cream paper layer. We applied a multiply-blended paper texture to this layer, perfectly mimicking the tactile feel of an art book or journal.\n*   **Floral Centerpiece Detail**: While the \"page\" is actively turning across your screen (and the site scrolls underneath it), a subtle, faded leaf emblem encased in a delicate border appears in the center, labeled gently with the word \"Chapter\" to enhance the physical reading experience.\n*   **Synchronized Lenis Scrolling**: The smooth scrolling (`ReactLenis`) logic is now integrated into this transition timing. The engine waits until the screen is fully obscured by the new page before initiating the scroll, ensuring you never see a jarring jump\342\200\224only a smooth reveal as the new page completes its turn. \n\nAll main navigational anchors across the navigation bar, hero buttons, and footer links have been wired into this new transition system."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 2
      nanos: 646741319
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
