  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Aesthetic**\n\nI\'m initially focusing on understanding the user\'s desire for a sophisticated, floral preloader. The goal is to achieve a seamless page entry with a fading-out animation. I\'m visualizing the desired aesthetic to ensure the animation complements the caf\303\251 experience.\n\n\n**Outlining the Structure**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the practical implementation. I\'ve broken it down into a new `Preloader.tsx` component that will overlay the screen. It should feature floral elements, elegant typography, and possibly a loading percentage. The preloader will listen to a `window.onload` event, or a timeout to control its display duration. Finally, it must fade out gracefully. I\'m exploring background color options, settling on a dark teal or ivory.\n\n\n**Refining the Implementation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the specifics. I am leaning towards a central floral SVG or the caf\303\251 logo animated with Framer Motion. I\'ll probably use an SVG from `lucide-react` such as the `Flower2` or `Leaf`, or I\'ll create a simple custom SVG. The text \"Blueberrys Caf\303\251\" in a serif, italic mix feels right. I\'m considering an elegant progress line for the loading indicator. The next step is creating the `Preloader.tsx` component and utilizing `framer-motion` to handle the animations.\n\n\n**Implementing the Code**\n\nI\'ve begun building the `Preloader` component using React, incorporating `lucide-react` for the floral icon and `framer-motion` for animations. I\'m leveraging `useState` to manage the loading state and I\'ve integrated `useLenis` to pause and resume the smooth scrolling during the preloader\'s display. I\'m using `useEffect` hooks to handle the preloader display duration, setting a minimum time or waiting for the `window` to load. I am now using `Promise.all` to combine the `minimum time` and the `window load` event before setting `isLoading` to false. The loading indicator is still underway.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Component**\n\nI\'ve now finalized the preloader component\'s animation and structure. I focused on a graceful exit animation, with the preloader sliding upwards as it fades out. Within the component, there\'s a central container, a delicate floral icon, and a progress bar scaling from 0 to 1 over 2 seconds. I also made sure \"Blueberrys Caf\303\251\" fades in subtly. I\'ve placed the component at the top of the `App.tsx` tree and checked the dependencies. The component is complete.\n\n\n**Refining the Lifecycle**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on managing the preloader\'s display with `useLenis`, specifically pausing and resuming smooth scrolling based on the `isLoading` state. I\'ve ensured a minimum display time of 2.5 seconds using a `Promise` combined with a `window` load check, so the page doesn\'t just flicker. I\'m leveraging `Promise.all` for a more reliable, smoother loading experience. I want to guarantee the preloader always finishes gracefully.\n\n\n**Animating the Element**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a custom floral SVG animation within the preloader. I\'m using Framer Motion to create a path animation for a drawing. The `opacity` also fades in, creating a more engaging entrance. I\'m leveraging a subtle background texture to add depth.\n\n\n**Refining the Animation**\n\nI\'ve just added a Framer Motion SVG path animation for a drawing of three flower stems, with an opacity fade-in on a delay. I\'ve also added a subtle \"Blueberrys Caf\303\251\" text fade in below it, improving the preloader\'s visual appeal. I\'ve used `easeInOut` easing to produce a smooth animation effect.\n\n\n**Integrating the Lenis Hook**\n\nI\'ve just ensured that the `useLenis` hook functions correctly within the preloader\'s lifecycle. I am now certain the smooth scrolling pauses and resumes as intended, providing a seamless transition. The loading indicator is also working. I\'m satisfied.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 37
      nanos: 96758357
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
