  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Parameters**\n\nI\'m currently focused on defining the project parameters. The goal is to incorporate gentle sound effects\342\200\224a soft page-turn and a subtle ambient bell\342\200\224to heighten the cinematic caf\303\251 experience. These sounds should be triggered by major interactive elements like navigation, menus, and buttons.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Sound Sources**\n\nI\'ve identified the specific sound files needed, like a page turn and a soft bell, for interactive elements. I\'m leaning toward the Web Audio API for synthesizing sounds due to reliability. Public domain URLs are an alternative, but it would be less seamless and controllable. I can\'t easily upload audio files and using external URLs runs the risk of breaking if the external URL is unavailable.\n\n\n**Constructing the Audio Engine**\n\nI\'m now building the audio logic, and I\'ve decided to prevent multiple `AudioContext` instances by using either a singleton `AudioEngine` or a React Context. I\'m leaning toward creating a utility file, `/src/lib/audio.ts`, to hold the synthesis logic. I\'ve also just remembered that browsers require user interaction to begin an `AudioContext`, the first click or tap will resume the context. I\'m planning to initialize the audio context on first user interaction.\n\n\n**Synthesizing Sound Effects**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the sound synthesis using the Web Audio API. I\'m focusing on creating an elegant page-turn sound with white noise and a sine wave with specific frequency modulations and gain envelopes. I\'m carefully constructing the sound using `createBuffer` and modulating it to get just the right effect. I\'m also ensuring the audio context is properly connected to the destination.\n\n\n**Refining the Audio Implementation**\n\nI\'m now refining the audio implementation, creating a `BufferSource` and employing a BiquadFilter for a bandpass effect. I\'m focusing on the bandpass filter\'s frequency modulation with careful gain envelopes for a realistic sound. I\'ve also incorporated a sine wave oscillator to make the chime sound, which also uses a gain node connected to the destination.\n\n\n**Implementing the Chime**\n\nI\'ve just added the chime sound effect, defining its frequency and gain envelopes to create a gentle, sustained tone. I\'ve set the frequency to 1200 Hz initially, then controlled the gain to ramp it up and then down, adding the start and stop times. I\'m now integrating this into the audio engine and ensuring the sound is connected to the destination for playback. I\'m also ensuring the audio context is properly connected to the destination.\n\n\n**Integrating Hover Effects**\n\nI\'ve made great progress! I\'ve now integrated audio feedback for interactive elements. I\'ve updated `CustomCursor.tsx` to play a hover sound on mouse over and made sure to initialize the audio context first. This involved checking the interaction target and using `useEffect` for state management, it\'s elegant and clean. I\'ve also added a page turn sound in `TransitionContext.tsx` to play on navigation. Finally, the full menu now plays a chime to indicate it\'s opening.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Integration**\n\nI\'m now implementing the full audio system! I\'m creating the `/src/lib/audio.ts` file and updating `CustomCursor.tsx`, `TransitionContext.tsx`, `MenuSection.tsx`, and `MenuOverlay.tsx`. I\'ve defined `playHover()` for a subtle tick sound. The `playPageTurn()` function has a white noise burst, and `playChime()` uses two sine oscillators. I\'m focusing on ensuring these integrate seamlessly.\n\n\n**Adjusting the Sound**\n\nI\'m now refining the hover sound\'s code, aiming for a less harsh tick. I\'ve switched the oscillator to sine wave and adjusted the frequency. I\'m focusing on decay and am attempting to prevent harshness in the sound synthesis.\n\n\n**Refining the Page Turn**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the page-turn effect. I\'ve adjusted the white noise amplitude for a gentler sound. I\'m also attempting to create a realistic sliding sound by using a bandpass filter sweep. My current code uses a 400ms buffer filled with noise, with the filter frequency swept between 600Hz and 1200Hz.\n\n\n**Optimizing the Audio**\n\nI\'ve just been working on the chime, making it less sharp. The code now reduces the initial gain and ramps it more gently. I\'m also attempting to create a realistic sliding sound by using a bandpass filter sweep on the page turn. It\'s using a 400ms buffer filled with noise, with the filter frequency swept between 600Hz and 1200Hz.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 28
      nanos: 437167341
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
